      {
        "id": 225418,
        "name": "The Mariner",
        "username": "The_Mariner",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/the_mariner/{size}/2025_2.png",
        "created_at": "2019-01-31T15:10:12.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>The following page has links to all the templates, fonts, etc. you need:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://boardgamegeek.com/thread/813176/card-templates\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">https://boardgamegeek.com/thread/813176/card-templates</a></p>\n<p>As for me, I use GIMP to actually make the cards. &nbsp;It took a while to get things just right, but if you want I can send you the .xcf of one of my later cards with all the formatting done fairly&nbsp;well. &nbsp;I don’t have the gradient in the title text, though- there’s a way to do that, but I haven’t bothered, since my cards don’t even have real art.</p>\n<p>And for font needs for titles and such, I use <a href=\"http://1001freefonts.com\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">1001freefonts.com</a>.</p>\n<p>And lastly, some design notes: if you don’t have art, please use stock photos. &nbsp;It makes a world of difference in keeping your cards visually distinguishable from each other. &nbsp;Secondly, don’t worry too much about balance on heroes- there’s a very broad spectrum of power levels in SotM heroes. &nbsp;Focus on just weeding out infinite loops and getting to a general power level. &nbsp;Villains are another story, and you may need to playtest and tweak them nonstop for several iterations. &nbsp;My suggestion is to already know what difficulty you want. &nbsp;Environments also have a broad spectrum, and are the most contentious. &nbsp;Some people like environments that do a lot and force you to deal with them; some people hate those kinds of environments and think that environments should only have a few “oh no” cards.</p>\n<p>Lastly, playtesting in this game is based mostly on “feel.” &nbsp;With only a few trials you can’t get Sentinels-Statistics-Project levels of data. &nbsp;So when balancing, try to focus on how your deck feels. &nbsp;Remember- it needs to not only be balanced, it needs to be fun. &nbsp;I’ve had to take more decks back to the drawing board because they weren’t fun than because they weren’t balanced.</p>",
        "post_number": 4,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:59:02.701Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
        "quote_count": 0,
        "incoming_link_count": 8,
